GNU QUIC NEWS - history of user-visible changes.

* Noteworthy changes in release ?.? (????-??-??) [?]

** Initial development.  No release has been made.
** Version negotiation for TLS and DTLS (tlsauto.h, dtlsauto.h): by
default a server chooses between the TLS 1.3 and TLS 1.2 engines (or DTLS
1.3 and 1.2) from the ClientHello, so one listener serves both and
prefers 1.3, and a client sends one hello offering both and falls back if
the server picks 1.2. The version is decided once; there is no
renegotiation into another. A 1.2 server that also serves 1.3 sets the
RFC 8446 downgrade sentinel and negotiating clients enforce it. Either end
can be pinned to one version (for a port per version, and tests). QUIC
stays TLS 1.3 only. In a combined hello the 1.2 client accepts X25519
and secp384r1 ECDHE curves as well as secp256r1 (only when it also
offered 1.3). The interop tools use the negotiating front.
** 0-RTT (RFC 9001 section 4.6): a client that resumes a session sends
stream data and datagrams in its first flight under the remembered server
limits; a server accepts them once per ticket (single use replay cache)
and tells the application when data is early (gq_conn_early_data_active).
Rejected early data is sent again in 1-RTT packets; a server that
tightened its limits is refused. The endpoint can keep the ticket keys
and replay cache. Tested in simulation (loss, reordering, v1 and v2) and
against quiche in both directions. Also fixes the client's early
traffic secret emission in the TLS engine, which was never defined.
** DATAGRAM frames (RFC 9221) with acknowledgement and loss notification,
and the endpoint layer (endpoint.h): connections behind one socket are
routed by connection ID, new clients are admitted (Version Negotiation,
Retry always or when busy, tokens), unknown connections are answered
with rate limited stateless resets whose tokens derive from a key, and
timers are kept in a heap so idle connections cost nothing. Tested
in simulation and against quiche (four clients at once, datagrams both
ways).
** Path validation and connection migration (RFC 9000 sections 8.2 and 9):
gq_conn_recv_path and gq_conn_send_path carry opaque addresses; a server
follows a peer that moves (validating the new path, limiting what it sends
there to three times what arrived, falling back if validation fails), a
client can probe paths and migrate (gq_conn_probe_path, gq_conn_migrate),
and each path uses its own peer connection ID.  Tested in a simulated NAT
and against quiche's migration in both directions.
** QUIC version 2 (RFC 9369) is now the default preference: endpoints
list v2 then v1, a client starts in the oldest listed version and the
server follows the client's order (RFC 9368), so two endpoints of this
library talk v2. Session tickets are bound to the QUIC version (the
server derives a separate ticket key ring for v2, RFC 9369 section 5), and
the conn ticket event reports the version.
** Version negotiation: RFC 9368 version_information transport parameter,
compatible negotiation between QUIC v1 and v2 during the handshake with
downgrade protection, and the client's restart after a Version
Negotiation packet (RFC 9000 section 6.2).
** Address validation (token.h, listen.h): Retry and NEW_TOKEN tokens
bound to the client address, a stateless admission step that answers
Version Negotiation and Retry, and client handling of Retry; tested
against quiche.
** QUIC connections (conn.h) and streams (stream.h, ranges.h): the
handshake, packet number spaces, ACKs, RTT estimation, loss detection and
probe timeouts and NewReno congestion control with persistent
congestion (RFC 9002), stream and
connection flow control, connection IDs, key update, idle timeout,
connection close and stateless reset; tested against quiche in both
directions, with loss.
** DTLS 1.2 (dtls12.h, dtls12cookie.h): the TLS 1.2 profile over datagrams,
with whole-flight retransmission and a stateless HelloVerifyRequest;
tested against OpenSSL, GnuTLS and wolfSSL.
** DTLS 1.3 (dtls.h, dtlscookie.h): the TLS 1.3 engine over datagrams with
record number encryption, fragmentation, ACKs, retransmission, KeyUpdate
and a stateless address-validating cookie; tested against wolfSSL.
** TLS 1.2 (tls12.h, tls12conn.h) with the same restricted profile as
Gumdrop and Hopf: ECDHE on secp256r1 and AEAD suites only, mandatory
extended master secret and renegotiation indication, ticket resumption.
